# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 1, ATTEMPT 3 on bagisto. THIS IS A RE-QUESTION, routed back from two measured
# two-arm mini-benches. The anchor is UNCHANGED (Webkul\Product\Models\Product); only the
# question and the gold group move, per ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR.
#
# TRAJECTORY - EVERY previous cycle on this repo, and which side of the window each fell on.
# The window is: plain arm <= 0.50 AND ours >= plain + 0.50. Both at once.
#   $ cat verticals/php-laravel/results/loop/bagisto/cycles.jsonl
#   {"cycle": 1, "attempt": 1, "phase": "minibench", "verdict": "REQUESTION",
#    "baseline_dependents": 0.6471, "sense_dependents": 0.7647,
#    "title": "Bagisto work session - audit what holds a product it never names", ...}
#   {"cycle": 2, "attempt": 2, "phase": "minibench", "verdict": "REQUESTION",
#    "baseline_dependents": 0.8235, "sense_dependents": 0.8235,
#    "title": "Bagisto work session - audit the kind-specific slices of the catalogue record", ...}
#   (results/dryrun/bagisto/ holds minibench.1.md only - no archived cycles.*.jsonl exists,
#    checked this session, so the two lines above are the COMPLETE history.)
#
#   cycle 1  baseline 0.6471  sense 0.7647  delta +0.1176
#            side: BASELINE-TOO-HIGH. Failed the <=0.50 condition on its own. Arithmetic
#            killer: recall caps at 1.00, so a baseline at 0.6471 caps the achievable delta
#            at +0.3529 - dead before it ran.
#            Route: one accessor sweep, `grep -rn "\->product\b"`, widened four times, then
#            confirmation reads. The plain arm was CUT OFF at its hard cap (292 s of 291 s,
#            exit 124) with 11 of 17 cited, so 0.6471 is a floor.
#   cycle 2  baseline 0.8235  sense 0.8235  delta +0.0000
#            side: BASELINE-TOO-HIGH AGAIN, and further out than cycle 1. The correction
#            over-shot in the same direction rather than crossing to the far side.
#            Route: `grep -rn` over a seven-term disjunction of the per-kind collection
#            names, which printed the candidate set WITH path:line already attached.
#            Second defect, equally fatal: the sense arm made 2 MCP calls all run
#            (sense_search, sense_status), NO sense_blast and NO sense_graph, then fell back
#            to grep and ran the baseline's route. The pair measured one route twice.
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE.
# Correcting from BASELINE-TOO-HIGH, for the third time, and this draft moves on the two
# things both previous cycles got wrong rather than on the last one's complaint alone:
#   (a) NO GOLD ROW MAY BE THE TERMINUS OF A SINGLE DISJUNCTIVE GREP THE ASK SUPPLIES. Cycle 1
#       handed over the accessor by asking "which places hold one of these records"; cycle 2
#       handed over seven collection names by asking which kind-specific slice each site owns.
#       This draft's ask names no relation, no collection, no branch value and no category of
#       subsystem: it asks by which ROUTE the record ARRIVED at each site.
#   (b) THE SENSE ARM MUST HAVE A REASON TO ASK THE STRUCTURAL QUESTION. Step 2 is written as
#       a change-impact audit on a symbol step 1 forces the arm to name, and step 1 explicitly
#       forbids enumerating the far side, so the arm arrives at step 2 holding a class name
#       and nothing else. That is the only entry point this loop can build without naming a
#       tool (banned by the neutrality gate).
# Intended landing: baseline in the 0.20-0.40 band, sense at or above 0.75. The guard against
# over-shooting to the far side ("neither arm gets anything") is that all 18 rows below sit
# inside a `direct_callers` row of the SHOWN blast payload with the enclosing routine spelled
# out, and sense reached 13 of 17 in cycle 1 on a harder set while finishing in 242 s of 480.
#
# RE-GOLDED FROM WHAT THE PLAIN ARM MISSED CONSISTENTLY, NOT FROM ONE SLOPPY RUN.
# The two cycles ran DIFFERENT gold sets that overlap in 7 ids. Cross-tabbed by hand from the
# two inverse_frequency runs (scoped to the headline model's results root):
#   $ python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py bagisto verticals/php-laravel/results/claude-opus-5/minibench
#   $ python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py bagisto ... --scenario sha256:d5cc59c3833a3733
#   taken by the plain arm in BOTH cycles : d:flat-indexer-refresh  (1/1 and 1/1)  -> CUT
#   taken in cycle 2 only, i.e. only once the seven collection names were in the ask:
#     d:admin-grouped-options, d:admin-product-form, d:admin-product-resource,
#     d:admin-shipment-inventory, d:booking-price-note, d:importer-type-family
#     - all six were 0/1 to the plain arm in cycle 1, whose ask did not name them -> KEPT,
#       and they are the measured core of this group.
#   never cited by either arm, either cycle: d:repo-media-filetype, d:type-bundle-children,
#     d:type-configurable-children -> KEPT. Checked against the payload, not assumed: all
#     three appear in `direct_callers` (ProductMediaRepository\resolveFileTypeQueryBuilder
#     104-113, Type\Bundle\getChildrenIds 148-151, Type\Configurable\getChildrenIds 294-297),
#     so the tool CAN serve them and "hard" is the right reading, not "unreachable".
#   cited by both arms in cycle 1, the storefront/checkout/sales read path (cart facade, cart
#     model, order model, both shop resources, the shop API controller, the three sales item
#     repositories, the configurable-option variant helper) -> all CUT. They are the accessor
#     sweep's own output and they put the 0.6471 floor there.
# n IS 1 PER ARM PER CYCLE AND THAT BOUNDS THIS. The rule is not to re-gold below ~5 runs per
# arm because the ordering is noise there, so no row was cut on the RANKING alone. Every cut
# above is made by the QUESTION changing; the ranking is quoted as corroboration. The one
# exception is deliberate and stated: d:flat-indexer-refresh is cut on 2 independent
# observations under 2 DIFFERENT asks, which is the "missed/taken CONSISTENTLY" test the plan
# asks for and not a single-run read.
#
# CONFOUND, NAMED AND RUN. "The plain arm misses the admin/importer/add-on periphery" co-varies
# with three other things in cycle 1's single transcript: those rows are reached LATE in the
# sweep, the run was CUT OFF at its cap, and they sit in packages the sweep filtered. One
# transcript cannot separate them. Cross-tab against cycle 2 does separate one: in cycle 2 the
# plain arm reached ALL SIX of those same rows and was NOT cut off - so "area" alone does not
# predict the miss, and what changed between the two runs is the ASK. That is the finding this
# draft is built on; wall pressure and sweep order remain live and are what the next run
# measures.
#
# THE AXIS - AND THE ONE SENTENCE THE PLAN ASKS FOR
# The mechanism: at almost none of these sites is the record a thing anyone declared. It
# arrives by one of a small number of indirect routes - one hop in from a row of a
# neighbouring table under a name that is not its own (`$option->associated_product`,
# `$bundleOptionProduct->product`, `$child->product`); forwarded through a neighbouring row's
# OWN method, so the line that depends on the record does not mention it at all
# (`$item->isStockable()` at OrderRepository.php:220, which OrderItem.php:70 forwards to the
# record's per-kind object); handed back by an object the record itself built and populated
# (Product.php:359 `$this->typeInstance->setProduct($this)`); pulled out of an untyped bag by
# string key (PriceNote.php:16); built unsaved purely to be interrogated (Importer.php:2621,
# under the alias `ProductModel`); or passed as an argument with no declared type anywhere in
# the signature. The record's own file, its proxy, its contract and its write path are the
# only places where the relationship is written down, and they are the non-scoring anchors.
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS: it is, per site, the
# PROVENANCE of the record - by which of those routes it got there and at which line the
# handle was established, which is a fact about how the site obtained the record and is
# frequently NOT visible on the line that consumes it. An occurrence list prints the lines
# that mention a name; it cannot state where a handle came from, and three of the rows below
# (OrderRepository.php:220, ShipmentController.php:121, ProductResource.php:42) carry no
# occurrence of the record's name on the cited line at all.
#
# THE KIND (DECIDE, "the kind of question")
# The possession kind - who holds a handle and where it was established - not the occurrence
# kind. It is on the menu because step 0b's ring is NON-EMPTY, measured this session:
#   $ python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#   Order    class 31 rows 31 files 15 vias  CANDIDATE -> scenario material (Loop 3)
#   Product  class 31 rows 31 files 15 vias  CANDIDATE -> scenario material (Loop 3)
# RECORDED, NOT USED: the ring itself is not served to the arm. The shown payload carries
# `retained_via_interfaces_count: 31` with `retained_trimmed: true` and NO rows, so no gold row
# below is taken from the ring. Every row comes from `direct_callers`, which IS shown.
#
# SEAM PROFILE - three candidates profiled (step 2), lowest precision first.
# PRECISION = structural dependent files / grep-hit files; LOWER IS BETTER.
#   $ python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Order Order --file packages/Webkul/Sales/src/Models/Order.php
#     app caller files : 13    SCATTER (dirs) : 10
#     GREP-NOISE : 'Order' in 131 app files            PRECISION=0.10   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   $ python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Product Product --file packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php
#     app caller files : 35    SCATTER (dirs) : 17
#     GREP-NOISE : 'Product' in 308 app files          PRECISION=0.11   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   $ python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE ProductRepository ProductRepository --file .../ProductRepository.php
#     app caller files : 15    SCATTER (dirs) : 8
#     GREP-NOISE : 'ProductRepository' in 49 app files PRECISION=0.31   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
# Product is KEPT (the re-question rule keeps the anchor) and the profile agrees: 0.11 against
# Order's 0.10 is inside noise, and Order yields 13 dependent files where Product yields 35, so
# Order cannot fill eighteen rows across eleven areas. 0.11 sits in the band the calibration
# names (a banked +1.00 cell profiled at 0.078). Precision RANKS; it never kills, and no grep
# census was run against any row below.
# SCRIPT NOTE (one line, not fixed, per SCOPE): seam_hunt.py defaults its grep token to the
# resolved LANGUAGE when `--file` is given without a positional token, reporting
# "token='php' in 2078 app files PRECISION=0.00 RESOLVER-GAP?" for every symbol. Passing the
# token positionally is the working invocation and is what is quoted above.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN (step 3), at MCP defaults, no min_confidence passed:
#   $ python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE \
#       '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Product","file":"packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php"}}]'
#   risk high, risk_factors ["93 direct callers"], direct_callers 55 rows over 35 FILES,
#   affected_symbols 310, production_affected 130, affected_via_composition 25,
#   importers_not_reached 3, truncated true, snippets_truncated true.
#   ALL 18 dependents rows below sit inside a shown `direct_callers` row; the enclosing
#   [def start-end] is recorded on each row and was checked against the payload, not assumed.
#
# MEMORIZATION (step 5), and what it changes here:
#   $ python3 bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Product --json /tmp/mem.json
#   ok:false  reason: "closed-book recall 0.458 (33 members named) >= 0.3 - recited"
#   hits: attribute_family, attribute_values, bundle_options, catalog_rule_prices, categories,
#         channels, cross_sells, customer_group_prices, downloadable_links,
#         downloadable_samples, getTypeInstance, grouped_products, images, inventories, ...
# What is recited is the RECORD'S OWN MEMBER SURFACE, and every one of those members sits in
# the non-scoring `contract` group. But this is not cosmetic: it means cycle 2's seven-term
# disjunctive grep was available to the plain arm CLOSED-BOOK, without the ask leaking it. So
# gold rows whose pinned line is a bare read of a recited member are free whether or not the
# ask names them. Two such rows are kept ONLY because they are measured 0/2 across both arms
# and both cycles (d:type-bundle-children, d:type-configurable-children) plus the media
# resolver; every other candidate of that shape was cut for this reason.
# ============================================================================

name: Bagisto work session - audit how the catalogue record arrives where it is used
repo: bagisto
contract_symbol: Webkul\Product\Models\Product
contract_file: packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer of this store platform, about to rework the record that the whole
  catalogue hangs off - the single most-reached-for thing in the codebase. The change is a
  narrowing: after it, code will no longer be able to obtain one of these records implicitly.
  Anything that ends up working on one will have to have been handed it explicitly, and every
  place that today gets one by some other means has to be found and rewritten first.
  The difficulty is not that these records are used in many places. It is that in most of them
  nobody wrote down that a record was obtained at all. A routine is handed a row of some
  neighbouring table and steps across to a record from it, under a name that belongs to the
  neighbour rather than to the record. Another asks a neighbouring row a question the
  neighbour quietly forwards, so the line that depends on the record does not mention it. One
  is handed a record back by an object the record itself built and populated. One takes it out
  of an untyped bag by string key. One builds an unsaved one purely to interrogate it, under a
  different name entirely. Several receive one as an argument with no declared type on it
  anywhere in the signature, and one IS the record and refers to itself.
  So the question the rework turns on is provenance: at each place, by what route did a record
  get here, and at which line was the handle established. That question is not answered by
  knowing where a name appears - at several of these places the record's name appears nowhere
  on the line that depends on it, and at several others the name that does appear belongs to
  something else.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: what the record is, where it is declared,
  what the one layer whose stated job is to make and change these records is, and how the
  record hands itself to the object that speaks for it; and then, separately, every place
  downstream that obtains a record by one of the indirect routes and would break under the
  narrowing, with the route named and the line where the handle is taken.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed place is a broken storefront shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the catalogue record contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it. Three things, and the first is the largest.
      First, the record itself. Name it precisely - the fully qualified name of the class and
      the exact file:line it is declared at - and say what code that holds one can actually do
      with it, grouped by job rather than listed routine by routine: what it relates outward
      to, and how it answers for a field that was never declared on it. Every routine you name
      gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without that location
      does not count. This platform gives the same short name to more than one thing, so say
      which one you settled on and how you told them apart, at file:line each.
      Second, the two places where getting hold of one of these records is written down openly.
      One is the layer whose stated job is to make, change and delete them: name it at
      file:line and say in a phrase what it is responsible for. The other is the record handing
      ITSELF outward - the exact line where it builds the object that speaks for it and gives
      that object a handle back on itself, plus the base class that object extends, at
      file:line, and which part of that base's state is the record. Say how the handle gets
      there, since nothing on the receiving side establishes it.
      Third, and deliberately small: say once, briefly, what it looks like when a routine
      somewhere else has obtained one of these records WITHOUT any of that being visible - no
      declared type, no name of the record on the line, the handle taken from something whose
      own subject is different. One example, located at file:line, with the line where the
      handle is actually taken. Do not enumerate those places here; I am not asking for that
      list yet and it earns nothing.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The record named precisely with its declaring file:line and disambiguated from the same-named siblings, its own surface grouped by job with each routine located at file:line, the declared write layer pinned at file:line with what it is responsible for, the exact line where the record builds the object that speaks for it and hands that object a handle back on itself, the base class that object extends pinned at file:line with the part of its state that is the record, and one located example of a routine that obtained a record by an indirect route
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the catalogue record contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. Take it as settled that the narrowing is happening: after it, no
      routine may end up working on one of these records unless it was handed one explicitly.
      Answer the only question that matters after that - which routines in this codebase would
      break, and by what route does each of them get hold of a record today.
      The unit of the answer is a site, not a package and not a file, and a single file can
      hold more than one. The record itself, the layer whose job is to create and update these
      records, and the base class of the object that speaks for a record are not answers here:
      you pinned those in the first part and naming them again earns nothing.
      These sites will not look like what you are searching for, and that is the whole
      difficulty. At several of them the record's own name does not appear on the line that
      depends on it: the routine holds a row of some neighbouring table and asks that
      neighbour a question the neighbour quietly forwards onward. At several others a name
      does appear on the line, but it belongs to the neighbour rather than to the record, so
      what is written there says nothing about what is actually held. One site IS a record and
      refers to itself. One takes a record out of an untyped bag by string key. One builds an
      unsaved one purely to interrogate it, under an alias. And several receive one as an
      argument carrying no declared type anywhere in the signature, so nothing in the shape of
      the routine says a record is in play at all.
      For each site the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line and the
      name of the routine it sits in; the ROUTE by which a record reached that site, said
      concretely - through which neighbour, forwarded by which method, taken from which
      argument or which bag; and the line where the handle was actually established, which
      will often be in a different file from the site itself. Keep it short: I want the
      coverage, the route and the line the handle comes from, not the prose.
      Organise by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished only when every
      site inside it is listed separately with its own location. If you run short, say what
      you had still not looked at rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no
      route named is half an answer, and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a group
      with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every site located at the exact file:line with the routine it sits in named, the route by which a record reached that site said concretely, and the line where the handle was established
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the record itself, its empty marker interface, the swap-in proxy, the
  # ---- declared write path and the base of the per-kind family. Step 1's answer,
  # ---- deliberately NON-SCORING. A FREE ROW IS A GIFT TO THE BASELINE, and these are
  # ---- exactly the rows both arms reach in one lookup - and the ones memorization_probe
  # ---- shows this model already recites closed-book (recall 0.458 over Product's member
  # ---- list, 33 of 72 members named). Putting them here removes the floor they would
  # ---- otherwise put under the plain arm. Every line below was opened and read this
  # ---- session. None appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-product
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Models/Product.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/Product.php:25 `class Product extends Model implements ProductContract` - the contract itself; :347 `public function getTypeInstance(): AbstractType` builds the class name from the stored kind at :353 `$this->typeInstance = app(config('product_types.'.$this->type.'.class'));`, throws at :356 when the stored value matches nothing, and hands ITSELF outward at :359 `$this->typeInstance->setProduct($this);` - the one line that establishes the handle every per-kind object holds; :382 `public function getAttribute($key)` is the override that answers for a field never declared on the model"
  - id: c:product-contract
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Contracts/Product.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Contracts/Product.php:5 `interface Product {}` - an EMPTY marker interface, implemented by the model at Product.php:25 and carrying no members at all, so nothing can be learned about the contract by reading it; it exists so the concrete class can be swapped, and it is one of the three things in this repo answering to the same short name"
  - id: c:product-proxy
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Models/ProductProxy.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Models/ProductProxy.php:7 `class ProductProxy extends ModelProxy {}` - the swap-in indirection every relation declaration writes instead of the model class (`ProductProxy::modelClass()`); it is the token a plain search reaches for first and the reason a neighbouring row's relation declaration never names the model"
  - id: c:product-repository
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Repositories/ProductRepository.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Repositories/ProductRepository.php:44 `public function model(): string` returning :46 `return Product::class;` and creating THROUGH the per-kind object at :56 `$typeInstance = app(config('product_types.'.$data['type'].'.class'));` then :58 `$product = $typeInstance->create($data);` - the declared write path named in step 1, non-scoring"
  - id: c:abstract-type
    group: contract
    match: [Product/src/Type/AbstractType.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Type/AbstractType.php:32 `abstract class AbstractType` holding :39 `protected $product;` (typed only in the docblock above it, `@var Product` at :37) - the receiving end of the handle established at Product.php:359, and the one holder whose grip on the record IS written down; pinned in step 1 so that step 2 is about the routes that are not"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: 18 files across 11 areas, one row per file. Each row is a site that
  # ---- obtains a record by a route nothing at the site declares. Every one is inside a
  # ---- `direct_callers` row of the SHOWN blast payload (enclosing def in brackets, checked
  # ---- against the payload), and every pinned line below was opened and read this session;
  # ---- the credit is what the line does, not a script tally.
  # ---- CUT from previous cycles and why: the whole storefront/checkout/sales READ path
  # ---- (cart facade, cart model, order model, both shop resources, the shop API controller,
  # ---- the three sales item repositories, the configurable-option variant helper) - cited by
  # ---- BOTH arms in cycle 1, it is the accessor sweep's own output. And
  # ---- d:flat-indexer-refresh, taken by the plain arm 1/1 in cycle 1 AND 1/1 in cycle 2
  # ---- under two different asks: two independent observations, which is the consistency
  # ---- test, not a single-run read.

  # -- area: the concrete per-kind classes. The abstract base is an anchor; these are not.
  # -- Route: the handle was pushed in from outside, at Product.php:359. Nothing here takes it.
  - id: d:type-bundle-children
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Type/Bundle.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Type/Bundle.php:150 `return array_unique($this->product->bundle_options()->pluck('product_id')->toArray());` inside `public function getChildrenIds()` at :148 [shown def 148-151] - the record is held in an inherited field it never assigns; the handle was established from the OUTSIDE at Product.php:359 `$this->typeInstance->setProduct($this)`, so no line in this file says where `$this->product` came from. MEASURED 0/2, both arms, both cycles - kept because the payload carries it, so it is hard and not unreachable"
  - id: d:type-configurable-children
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Type/Configurable.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Type/Configurable.php:296 `return $this->product->variants()->pluck('id')->toArray();` inside `public function getChildrenIds()` at :294 [shown def 294-297] - the same inherited field, the same externally-pushed handle, and a DIFFERENT sibling answer to the same question; the two files never reference each other and only the runtime lookup at Product.php:353 decides which one a caller reaches. MEASURED 0/2, both arms, both cycles"

  # -- area: the peripheral write repositories. Route: an untyped parameter, in one case with
  # -- the type named only in a docblock and in one case not named anywhere.
  - id: d:repo-media-filetype
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Repositories/ProductMediaRepository.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Repositories/ProductMediaRepository.php:107 `return $product->images();` inside `private function resolveFileTypeQueryBuilder($product, string $uploadFileType)` at :104 [shown def 104-113] - the record arrives as the ONE untyped parameter in a signature whose other parameter is typed `string`, and there is no docblock type either; the routine then branches on a string to choose WHICH collection on the record to write through (:107 images, :109 videos) and throws at :112 for anything else. MEASURED 0/2, both arms, both cycles"
  - id: d:repo-bundle-options
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Repositories/ProductBundleOptionRepository.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Repositories/ProductBundleOptionRepository.php:42 `$previousBundleOptionIds = $product->bundle_options()->pluck('id');` inside `public function saveBundleOptions($data, $product)` at :40 [shown def 40-67] - the record arrives untyped and undocumented as the SECOND parameter, after the array; the routine diffs existing ids against submitted ones and deletes the remainder at :64-:66, so it holds the record for the whole lifecycle of that collection with nothing in the signature saying so"

  # -- area: the read helpers that assemble option payloads.
  # -- Route: across from a neighbouring row, and a per-record memo cache.
  - id: d:helper-bundle-option-products
    group: dependents
    match: [Product/src/Helpers/BundleOption.php]
    relation: "packages/Webkul/Product/src/Helpers/BundleOption.php:97 `if (! $bundleOptionProduct->product->getTypeInstance()->isSaleable()) {` inside `private function getOptionProducts($option)` at :92 [shown def 92-123] - it never holds the outer record at all; it iterates the neighbouring option rows at :96 and steps ACROSS from each one to a second record, so the name on the line (`$bundleOptionProduct`) belongs to the neighbour and the handle is established by that neighbour's own relation declaration, in a different file"
